  "body": "## Accepted result and published preprint\n\n**Cyclic self-sumset recognition: a credited corollary of the integer hardness theorem**, Alec Kriebel. Published paper and verification package: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23430681 (https://zenodo.org/records/23430681).\n\nCredited exposition of the cyclic consequence of the integer hardness theorem of Abboud, Fischer, Safier, and Wallheimer. Ordinary all-pair self-sumset recognition in the same Z/nZ, with unbounded positive input n, is NP-complete separately for binary n with an explicit binary residue list and for an n-bit characteristic vector. Hardness holds already for odd n and nonempty B containing 0 with every canonical 2b<n. The universal proof gives integer normalization and an odd zero-anchor/no-wrap transfer; dense hardness uses the AFWS uniform 3SAT numerical bound 2^57(v+c)^4. A deterministic polynomial-time exact decision algorithm in either model exists iff P=NP. No new hardness gadget, novelty, first-publication, independent-discovery, or worldwide-openness claim is made. Extensive AI assistance was used in reasoning, source work, adversarial checking, coding, computational verification and writing. Unrefereed; no external human peer review or formal certification. Support includes byte-preserved original records and authored verification code, not third-party source bodies. Inspected AFWS proof: arXiv2410.18661v2 (26 October 2024); SODA2025 publication metadata separately verified.\n\n## Accepted scope and review\n\nSame-group ordinary cyclic self-sumset recognition is NP-complete in both explicit sparse binary and dense bit-vector encodings, already odd n, target zero-anchor and representatives <n/2.\n\nNo gap asserted for exact decision languages; unrestricted meanings of efficient, unconditional P!=NP, randomized/approximation/oracle claims excluded.\n\nThe result is accepted at the exact audited head `b7e55279707ce2c720304ca98111088d7d037371`. 2 fresh independent complete AI whole-package reviews were completed, with the final review finding zero actionable issues. Subsequent bounded administrative adoption is recorded in the current external ROOT final seal; earlier status/counter/hold fields remain dated history. Prior mathematical results and proof dependencies are credited. Historical novelty and worldwide current openness are unestablished. AI tools were used extensively; this is an unrefereed preprint, without conventional human peer review or formal proof certification.\n\nThe repository Zenodo uploader verified the exact reviewed metadata and PDF/support bytes. The Google Workspace CLI recorded the DOI/details in the requested tracker with exact read-back. No GitHub release is created. Current audit and publication receipts: `audits/pr1052_30005118`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Preserved original PR description: dated submission history</summary>\n\n## Conclusion and credit\n\nAdd the complete certificate and independent mathematical audit for 30005118 / OWR-10252930-031. Same-group cyclic self-sumset recognition is NP-complete in both explicit sparse binary and dense characteristic-vector encodings, already for odd n and nonempty zero-containing targets below n/2. A polynomial-time algorithm in either model exists if and only if P=NP. This is not an unconditional impossibility claim.\n\nInteger NP-hardness and the bounded-universe construction are credited to Abboud, Fischer, Safier, and Wallheimer, *Recognizing Sumsets is NP-Complete*, [arXiv:2410.18661v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18661v2), [SODA 2025](https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611978322.153). The elementary integer-to-cyclic transfer is supplied in full as a source-applicability argument without a novelty claim. No new hardness gadget construction or full independent audit of the source gadgets is asserted.\n\n## Full proof and audit\n\nThe certificate retains all universal mathematics: normalization with negative and odd minima, zero anchor, absence of 2-torsion, both no-wrap directions, total reduction including empty and zero-diameter cases, sparse bit complexity, dense bounded-universe composition and sparse NP certificate size. The restricted nonempty language uses the explicitly permitted fixed YES replacement (3,{0}). The audit retains every substantive source, mathematical and encoding check and all scope limits.\n\nOnly computational reproduction sections and obsolete or out-of-edition administrative framing are removed. All certificate proof sections and substantive audit sections are byte-identical to their reviewed originals. The source ledger is unchanged. The editions include a precise provenance account and acceptance tied to their actual bytes. AI-assisted and unrefereed; no external human peer review, exhaustive novelty certification or proof-assistant verification is claimed.\n\n## Repository boundary\n\nExactly eight files are added under `unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/30005118/`. No QUEUE.md, existing attempt, unrelated record, historical status or turn count changes. This edition preparation adds no proof-search turn and does not manufacture or reset accounting.\n\nThe packet includes authored proof and audit prose, scoped acceptance and public bibliographic verification metadata only. It excludes source copies or extractions, datasets, private material, scripts, fixtures and standalone outputs. No excluded artifact is transformed into new prose. No merge, release, DOI deposit or external outreach is part of this change.\n\n## Preparation checks\n\nExact editorial differences and byte-preserved mathematical blocks were checked. Public PDF byte/hash identities, UTF-8 and JSON, relative links, manifest, exact allowlist and excluded-material scan passed. The additive patch passed a clean temporary replay with exact path and byte matching. Base Git trees were recomputed and unrelated entries preserved. These are packaging checks, not further mathematical certification; no CI pass is claimed. Exact-head remote readback remains a separate publication check.\n\nBase: `f6caddda8f8be2cdb3e91494325d8f7f365bc3fc`.\n\n## Exact file identities\n\n- `ACCEPTANCE.md`: 2,804 bytes; SHA-256 `15d90535de3b08dc7c2eff6b83d1e26b96bba61ff8eb7d99f5dce467b819751f`\n- `CYCLIC_SUMSET_CERTIFICATE.md`: 11,272 bytes; SHA-256 `7f767eea9e2cf0e8bea67f3762d6116a0ac31d89f64cb2861499ba269d38c3a3`\n- `INDEPENDENT_AUDIT.md`: 6,928 bytes; SHA-256 `2683828c307ecdb427b102fbdd05d8c12b16774f4f2e4660617dcc9da171e319`\n- `MANIFEST.json`: 1,527 bytes; SHA-256 `e61bf10e64cf497c4ce0d46953eaf631e16da88919c3c982303e3dfac980192c`\n- `PROVENANCE.md`: 3,922 bytes; SHA-256 `6ad164c4f400dbbb704b3856a74fd4778c895fa4a760c5aaa2771886d2991827`\n- `README.md`: 2,574 bytes; SHA-256 `53f14c76b558ab9161d93e4b098321cb376443e74286c8543ed9364bb0f880e8`\n- `SOURCE_LEDGER.md`: 3,386 bytes; SHA-256 `91aad340ab6e86eb4b453dbbc64d52dc504fd84da4e540b08186f3fecf034728`\n- `STATUS.json`: 1,352 bytes; SHA-256 `5046e4e478b6c0d1ecbabd112655f2c0030ca1852f2212b39036d2a8f14e5834`\n\nThe manifest lists eight members and hashes the other seven; this body pins the manifest independently.\n\n\n</details>\n",
